WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:04.980
Hey hun!

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.060
Whatchya doin?

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.420
Oh just nailing my most valuable possession
to the wall.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:10.750
Jazzy Bear!

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.320
No, not the dog.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.980
My college diploma.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.600
Oh, right.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.600
Yep.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.250
This little piece of paper cost me about $320 per square inch.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.250
Wow.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.770
That’s almost as much as a Manhattan apartment.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.340
It does make you wonder...

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.700
I mean, would I be happier if I chose another
major?

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.360
Or gone to trade school instead??

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.820
At least you’d know you can’t hammer a
screw into the wall.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.720
Huh.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.300
I just thought it was fancy nail.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:48.780
[MUSIC]

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.800
It’s not exactly a news flash that college
is expensive.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.120
Graduates our age have an average of $37,172
in student loan debt--and they have to start

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.660
thinking about their own kids’ college.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:06.030
If Philip and I had a baby this year, at around
4% inflation per year, his or her in-state

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:10.940
tuition would run us about $162,000.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.700
There isn’t ONE explanation why tuitions
are inflating like a microwaved peep.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.140
But there are some strong theories.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.340
Supply and demand.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.460
Over the last 40 years, having an undergraduate
degree went from being considered an option

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.960
to a necessity, and the MORE people want something,
the more the people selling it can charge.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.970
Another theory puts the blame on student loans.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.140
Wait..how does that make sense?

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.960
Well, they’re scary easy to get.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.880
Colleges have no incentive to control costs
because they know the federal government and

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.869
other loan agencies will keep fronting the
money.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:54.760
And finally, administration costs have ballooned
as much as 60% in the last 25 years.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:00.340
A lot of that gets blamed on perks that colleges
are adding to attract customers--I mean, students.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.890
Davidson College offers free laundry service.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.750
Michigan Tech has an on-campus ski resort!

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:11.080
Of course, education is about more than just
dollars and cents, but there’s no denying

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.330
college is a financial investment.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:18.560
You’re paying all this money now in the
hopes that you’ll be able to make it back

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.480
later.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.160
So… will you?

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.700
For most people, yes.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.249
The question is how long it will take, and
that depends on your degree.

00:02:27.249 --> 00:02:30.599
So which majors pay for themselves the fastest?

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:34.739
Let’s RUN THE NUMBERS!

00:02:34.739 --> 00:02:35.739
 

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.020
We’ll start with the median income for somebody
with only a high-school diploma.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.779
Compare that to a college graduate with, say,
a Communications degree.

00:02:43.779 --> 00:02:46.459
That’s an extra $15,000 per year!

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:49.840
Not bad, but how much did it cost?

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:56.320
The average cost of tuition, room, and board
at an in-state university is $80,360.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.440
So our Communications major will break-even on
their investment in about 5.3 years.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.510
A Psychology Major 
will take 11 years to break even,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.349
an Arts Major will
take 8.8 Years, the most popular major, Business,

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:13.699
will break even in 3.2, and the most lucrative undergraduate

00:03:13.699 --> 00:03:18.799
major of all, Engineering, will pay for itself in just 2 years!

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:21.799
Okay, but what about graduate school?

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:27.069
While it’s true that some postgraduate degrees
can push your income into the next tax bracket

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:29.430
these percentages don’t tell the whole story.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.900
Take law school.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:38.159
The average law student graduates with somewhere
between $85,000 and $125,000 in extra debt

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:41.059
on top of their undergraduate loans.

00:03:41.059 --> 00:03:45.919
While the top students at the most prestigious
universities can pull down big bucks right

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.820
away, they’re in the minority.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:53.879
Almost half of law grads earn a median starting
salary of only $65,000 a year.

00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:59.549
That’s just a $15,000 pay bump for an extra
$100,000 of debt.

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:01.680
Okay, how about med school?

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.819
That’s gotta pay off big time, right?

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:10.000
Well, the median salary for a general practitioner
is $175,000 a year, but the average cost of

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.060
medical school at a public university is around
$200,000.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.870
When you factor in your undergraduate loans,
and the interest that debt is racking up while

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.300
you’re in residency, it’s very common
for doctors to end up paying almost half a

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.120
million dollars for 25 years for those two letters behind their name.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.560
If all this is sounding scary or discouraging,
just remember that statistically, it does

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.130
pay to go to college in the long run, whatever
your major.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.890
And there are things you can do early on that
will ease the debt burden down the road.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.820
SHOP AROUND.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:49.010
Most universities can supply you with information
about how much their alumni make in the workforce.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.960
They might even connect you with graduates
of your same major who can answer your specific

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.230
questions.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:58.000
And when comparing prices, make sure you factor
in the cost of living.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.870
Some cities are so expensive to live in it
can double the amount you’ll have to borrow.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.260
Consider community college.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.440
There’s a stigma that it’s only for people
who can’t get into “real” college, but

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.240
it’s actually a pretty smart decision.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:17.010
You can slash your undergraduate debt in half,
and not get locked into a lifelong financial

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.160
commitment at the wise old age of 18.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:25.410
That’s what Philip did, and today we have
the exact same diploma from the same university,

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.900
but mine cost more than twice as much.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.100
And lastly, look into alternative financing.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.490
There are companies and colleges that are
trying out a new-fangled approach called Income

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.890
Share Agreements in which the student agrees
to pay a fixed percentage of their salary

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.860
for a fixed period of time.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.040
Unlike a loan which relentlessly accrues interest
through good times and bad, ISA’s don’t

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.520
punish the borrower for making less money
than they hoped.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.630
They’re basically betting on you to do well,
and sharing some of the risk if you don’t.

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:59.150
Now, most people don’t choose their major
for purely economic reasons.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.590
People wanna do what they love and what they’re
good at, and no Powerpoint presentation is

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:05.340
gonna change their minds.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.570
And there are benefits to college that you
can’t quantify, like broadening your horizons,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.660
or meeting your future husband.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.730
Being money smart doesn’t mean basing your
life around money.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:20.520
It just means taking simple precautions early
on so you can chase your dreams without weights

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.820
around your ankles.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.300
And that’s our two cents.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:32.300
[MUSIC]

